  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a qualitative case study of a single, historically high-performing Indian city, which may limit direct generalizability to cities with different baseline institutional capacities.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the deceleration of residential rooftop solar adoption in Pune, India, using qualitative cross-stakeholder analysis mapped to a multilevel institutional framework. It traces how the market transitioned from early incentive-driven adoption among wealthy homeowners to a plateau characterized by interagency coordination gaps, regulatory shifts, and complex compliance procedures.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that sustaining decentralized energy transitions past the early-adopter phase depends on reducing administrative friction and stabilizing governance structures rather than simply increasing financial subsidies."
